title the glob was the guard, and eight witnesses stood outside the name
prompt Kyri as Dream: read KYRI and GAUGE_STYLE, honor the ITINERARY inner loop -- roster cold then hot, reds first, QA on touch, the council rota, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first -- pick up the running thread from the newest recommend line, and work the higher Glow rune ports lane under the standing adaptation grant.
think The prior lap's recommend pointed at my own lane -- the width ratchet in glow/rune_shape.rye -- yet reds-first orders the constructive queue behind the corrective one, and the card named an open ungated row. Of the six rows in the REDS pin, %230, %252, %255 and %281 wait at Keaton's door and %273 closed by reconstruction, which left %282 OPEN with one leg the prior lap could not close: caravan_subscribe_poll_signal RED on this pier, recorded as identical under A/B and standing outside caravan_suite. That last clause is the one I read twice. A witness outside the choir is a coverage question, and coverage questions are where a red hides rather than where it lives.
think The cold roster took the first half hour and I held still for it, reading rather than editing, since the runner digests the tree at open and close and refuses when they differ. It read 72 of 72 green, tree_moved=no -- cleaner than the prior lap's 70 of 71, whose one red was the stale nib of a lap that ended at git add. I also measured the remotes rather than pulling blind: xy stood at exactly my HEAD, so the twice-pulled rota's purpose was already satisfied and no rebase was owed.
think Council rota N mod 5 = 3 with N = git rev-list --count HEAD = 3528 -- Water, Venus, care and flow, whose sense is taste. It advanced by exactly one row from the prior lap's Fire, which is the cadence the grid was drawn for and does not always get. The row read as instruction three times over. Taste only works up close, so read the actual file and run the actual witness; the fixed seat holds that a seam wants two small answers, one on each side; and the cardinal seat asks what smaller thing already works and can this grow from it. The answer to that last question turned out to be sitting one file away.
think The measurement that mattered came before any repair. The Caravan choir's bijection globs caravan_*_witness.rish, and eight runners in tools/ca/ wear no such name -- the four subscribe_poll rings plus capabilities, restart_on_ok, seeds and witness_stop_footgun. They were never unregistered; they were invisible, so the guard written to catch an unheard witness printed ROSTER_OK truthfully at 113 of 113 while eight stood unheard. caravan/LADDER.md names all eight as the coverage for its earliest rungs, which are the ones every later rung composes over. My first grep for the class was far too blunt -- 398 tracked .rish files say GREEN without carrying _witness -- and reading them showed most are views, lap demos and launchers. Narrowed to Caravan's own room the class is exactly eight, and each of the eight calls itself a witness in its own opening line.
think Then the red itself, and it was not where the record said. Run through rishi the witness failed at its GREEN assertion; run command-for-command from bash it passed ten times out of ten. The difference was the witness's own shell. It opened rm -f A B && "$service" ... >"$out" 2>&1 & pid=$!, and & binds looser than && , so the whole AND-list backgrounds as one subshell and $! names that subshell. kill -TERM killed the wrapper, the service was orphaned to init and kept running, the wait loop fell through on its first check because the wrapper was already gone, and cat read an output file two lines deep. ps -eo pid,ppid,args showed the orphan at PPID 1 with two children of its own, which is the whole proof in one line.
think That also explains the prior lap's A/B honestly rather than dismissing it. The A/B compared the module's two arms and was correct to find them identical -- the fault was in the witness, which was the same in both arms. A comparison can be sound and still answer a question nobody asked.
obs The depth of the cut wanted deciding twice. For the shell, the minimum fix is one character -- && to ; -- and I went further, moving the probe into tools/fixtures/caravan_subscribe_poll_signal.sh, because the sibling ring's probe already had exactly that shape and a 300-character one-liner is what hid the fault. Growing from the smaller thing that already worked is the water row's cardinal question, and the answer was one directory over. For the coverage gap, the tempting cut was much deeper: parity_ch01.rish is the tree's top ungated aggregator at +107 witnesses by the 20260825.020027 survey, and putting it on the roster would have been a policy that survey deliberately declined to choose. That decision stays Keaton's; mine was bounded to the eight runners Caravan's own ladder already claims.
obs The reap earned its lines by failing first. My first refusal proof planted a service that traps SIGTERM: the probe refused at rc=2 in seven seconds against its 6.8-second budget, and left a sleep 60 at PPID 1, because a KILL on a parent never reaches what it spawned. The real service spawns two children, so the leak was the same shape. pgrep -P asks by parent rather than by command text, which matters here beyond tidiness -- a -f pattern would match this tree's own agent shell, a gotcha the pier has already charged twice.
obs Two guards went RED on me and both were right. phantom_path and tracked_link refused while my new fixture sat on disk and outside the index -- a cited file left untracked is exactly what they exist to catch, and it is the fault a lap that ends at git add keeps repeating. git add -A closed both. I also read my own count wrong once: the choir's roster appeared to register 114 against 113 on disk, and the extra was the suite's own path inside its run-line comment. The scan had it right and my grep did not.
obs My progress checks on the choir were wrong for twenty minutes. pgrep -f caravan_suite_witness matched my own shell's command line, so it answered still singing long after the choir had finished GREEN. The correct reading was in the output file the whole time. Measurement beats memory, and it beats a badly-shaped measurement too.
obs Grade-what-you-touch: the new fixture, the repaired witness, the choir header and the REDS shelf each read 100/100/100 composite A+ at Meter. caravan/LADDER.md reads 80 -- exactly B -- at its own declared Field setting, with register 50 over 838 sentences; B is the door, so it stands and pushed no frame. I did not re-grade it at Meter to buy a better number, which would have been reading the setting off the score rather than off the class.
obs The card fought its bound again. My first Now block ran 2,737 bytes against the old 2,606 and carried the card 124 over its 24,576; two trims brought it to 24,573, the same three bytes of room the REDS pin has. The ledger row was born on a shelf for that reason, the way %286 through %288 were, and the shelf filename carried rows- plural from the first keystroke -- the trap %288 named one row above.
obs The standing adaptation grant went unspent a third lap running. The one new file is shell because it must background a process and signal it by pid, which Rishi's synchronous run cannot express, and because its sibling probe is shell and a pair that must be read together should read alike. Custody gates were not approached: no key, no funds, no chain, no publish, no history rewrite, no collaborator surface.
obs The send found the tree had moved, and that is where the lap earned its last two lessons. I measured xy at the open -- it stood at my exact HEAD -- and measured it again before pushing, as the twice-pulled rota asks, and by then five commits had landed from the Sound pier. Reading their ledger before merging rather than after is what %252 taught, and it paid immediately: they had booked their own %289 at 20260827.024604 while mine was booked at 022424. The earlier stamp is mine, and the number is still theirs, because the seated resolution from the %283-%285 re-seat is that the push to xy IS the allocation and upstream's published number stands. Mine moved to %290. Fourth firing of that door; the derived-spine key still waits at Keaton's word, and four firings is the argument for it rather than a fifth row.
obs They found my red too, the same night, independently. Their commit closing %282 names the identical precedence fault, measured as 40851 against 40854, and repairs it with an exec before the service so the subshell becomes the supervisor. That is correct and I kept mine, for two reasons I can check rather than assert: exec leaves the timeout path returning exit 2 over a live supervisor, which is the leak that poisons the next run, and it leaves the 300-character one-liner standing, which is the form that hid the fault from both piers. The naming fault is a co-discovery and the row says so; the coverage gap is the half only this lap found, and it is the half that explains why the red went unseen at all.
obs The rebase conflicted on five files and one of them was the file I had renamed away -- a modify/delete where their exec edit stood against my deletion. Resolving that by rule rather than by reflex is the whole of it: my renamed file already carries a strictly stronger repair, so their edit is subsumed rather than lost, and I read their diff in full before saying so. The two generated pages were resolved by regenerating them rather than by hand-merging, since a tool owns those bytes. My Now block carried two claims that their laps had made false -- %282 open, %281's shadows unlocked -- and both were corrected rather than left standing.
obs The second re-integration is the one worth recording. xy moved again while I verified, and among the seven commits was `caravan: six exempt modules are witnessed on disk` -- the Sound pier reaching my exact finding independently, measuring it at 42 assertions standing outside the choir, and DEFERRING the repair by name: a rename stages sixteen index entries at once and a second session holds this checkout open, so it waits for one bench. Their design note at 20260827.034026 sizes the repair as eight renames, seven living repoints, eight roster registrations, and the header sentence cut. This lap is that repair landed, which means the note and the commit are one round done from two sides.
obs Their reading of the header was sharper than mine and I took it. I had cut the exemption sentence to bounded, chain, ladder_checks, parse_int, seed, service, tally_copy, twin; they measured that six of those -- bounded, capabilities, chain, seed, service, twin -- ARE witnessed, by the two runners standing in the same room, so the true helper list is three: ladder_checks, parse_int, tally_copy. Naming six witnessed modules as unwitnessed is precisely what made the gap read as settled rather than as drift, and my looser cut would have left that half-true. Where two readings disagree the second look wins, in public, and here the second look was not mine.
obs Their laps closed %230, %252, %255 and %281 on seated conventions while I worked, so the card's next-doors line I had written was already false when I wrote it. I took theirs and added the one door that is genuinely mine to hand over: the gating policy for parity_ch01 that %290 surfaces. A card shared by three stars goes stale between a read and a write, which is an argument for reading it again at the close rather than trusting the copy in hand.
